  UIBox1: {fileID: 0}
  UIBox2: {fileID: 0}
  UIBox1Text: {fileID: 0}
  UIBox2Text: {fileID: 0}
  water: {fileID: 1890791476}
  spray: {fileID: 134421753}
  vacuum: {fileID: 708108761}
  moveFoward: {fileID: 0}
  activeButtonColor: {r: 0.101960786, g: 0.8784314, b: 0, a: 1}
  deactivatedButtonColor: {r: 0.6509804, g: 0.50980395, b: 1, a: 1}
  buttons:
  - {fileID: 0}
  - {fileID: 0}
  - {fileID: 0}
  - {fileID: 0}
  - {fileID: 0}
  inAirText: "1) Ambient air \x14\x14\x14\x14\u2060\u2014 currently laden with record
    shattering levels of the climate altering gas carbon dioxide\x14 \u2060\u2014
    flows into the DACC machine. "
  sorbentText: 2) Inside the DACC are several panels of "sorbent," a material that
    bonds with CO2. The CO2 is captured in these panels and removed from the air.
    Over time, the panels become saturated with CO2.
  outAirText: 5) Air flows out of the DACC with less CO2 than when it entered, reducing
    the amount of greenhouse gas in the air.
  waterText: 3) Once the sorbent panels have been saturated, the box is sealed and
    water is sprayed on the panels. The water interacts with the sorbent, releasing
    CO2. Then the panels are ready to absorb more CO2.
  vaccumText: 4) After the CO2 is released from the panels, a vacuum sucks the CO2
    inside the sealed box and stores it in a canister. Once all the CO2 has been
    stored, the DACC reopens and the process repeats. The stored CO2 can be used
    in urban farms, where it is absorbed by plants and increases crop yield.
  overBox: 0
  overAirIn: 0
  overAirOut: 0
  overSorbent: 0
  overVaccum: 0
  overWater: 0
  currentState: 0
